_narrative":"Employs approximately 78K people. Revenue per employee of $488K ranks in the above the sector median among Industrials peers. Each employee generates $48K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 13\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (6/10).","financial_summary":["GE Vernova Inc. is a large-cap industrials business with a $194.6B market cap on $38.1B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +9% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size. Operating margins are expanding by +2.3pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at 8% against a 10% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and 19% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $3.7B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 10 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.02\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 26\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 1.8%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 310% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":3.1036124039790707},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $7.0M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-7039654.49},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"GE Vernova (GEV) Down 19.5% Since Last Earnings Report: Can It Rebound?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3036739/ge-vernova-gev-down-19-5-since-last-earnings-report-can-it-rebound?cid=CS-ZC-FT-realtime_blog-3036739","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795793414.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"GEV vs. AEP: Which Stock Is Better Positioned for Rising Power Demand?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3036308/gev-vs-aep-which-stock-is-better-positioned-for-rising-power-demand?cid=CS-ZC-FT-analyst_blog%7Cmost_popular_stocks-3036308","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795777680.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.34,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"How Is Surging